# Generated data. Note this must be `data/*`, not `data/` -- git cannot
# re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded, so an exception
# below would silently do nothing with a bare `data/`.
data/*

# ...except the fitted model weights. These are what actually define a
# given board: small, readable, and diffable. Since nflverse and ADP data
# shift daily, a board built last week can't be reproduced from scratch --
# but with the weights tracked there's a record of exactly what model
# produced it. This is the archive; the .xlsx is just a rendering of it.
!data/situational_weights.json

# ...and the mock draft transcriptions (Phase 13.6). Same argument as the
# weights, stronger: `data/*` exists because everything under it can be
# regenerated from the pipeline, and these cannot. They are 736 cells read
# by hand off two Sleeper boards that no longer exist anywhere, and they
# are the entire ADP feed the 32-team board now runs on. Losing them means
# re-transcribing from screenshots or going back to an FFC feed that dies
# in round 6.
#
# The derived files (mock_picks_32team.csv, mock_adp_32team.csv) stay
# ignored -- `python -m src.mock_adp` rebuilds both from these two.
!data/mock_boards/
!data/mock_boards/*.txt

# Same argument, same file type, added when Phase 12 created a second
# set of weights (Aug 6). Rookies are projected by a separate model
# against a separate baseline, so situational_weights.json alone no
# longer describes what produced a board.
!data/rookie_weights.json

# The out-of-sample validation record. Tracked because it is the EVIDENCE
# rather than an output: it names which features beat a constant on which
# held-out seasons, and build_board refuses to run without it. A weights
# file with no matching gate is a model nobody checked, and the point of
# keeping both is being able to see, later, that they matched.
!data/holdout_gate.json

# Phase 13.5. The playing-time model and ITS OWN gate, tracked for the
# same reasons as the three files above and paired for the same reason.
# `build_board.require_playing_time_gate` refuses to build if the model is
# present without a passing gate that is newer than it, so shipping one
# without the other produces a repo that cannot build a board.
#
# The universe and sweep CSVs are deliberately NOT tracked: both are
# regenerable from nflverse by `python -m src.playing_time`, and one of
# them is 115KB of row-level data that would churn on every refresh.
!data/playing_time.json
!data/playing_time_gate.json

# Draft boards are build artifacts now that src/build_board.py exists.
# Binary, useless diffs, and regenerable on demand -- delete old versions
# freely. Recover any previously committed one with:
#   git checkout <commit> -- 2026_Draft_Board_v7.xlsx
2026_Draft_Board_v*.xlsx

# Excel lock files (created while a workbook is open)
~$*

# Scratch file for long commit messages (`git commit -F COMMIT_MSG.txt`).
# Ignored because `git add -A` cheerfully committed one on Aug 6 -- the
# Remove-Item that was supposed to clean it up ran AFTER the commit.
COMMIT_MSG.txt

# Superseded draft boards. Only the CURRENT model version stays in the
# working tree -- older ones live in git history, where they can be
# recovered without sitting next to the real one in a folder you open
# under time pressure on draft day.
2026_*_Board_v[0-9].xlsx
2026_*_Board_v10.xlsx
2026_*_Board_v11.xlsx
2026_*_Board_v12.xlsx
2026_*_Board_v13.xlsx
2026_*_Board_v14.xlsx
2026_*_Board_v15.xlsx
2026_*_Board_v16.xlsx

# One line per version, deliberately, rather than a range like v1[0-9].
# A range would swallow the CURRENT board the moment the version counter
# reaches it, and the failure mode is silent: the board builds, the file
# lands, `git status` says nothing, and the only board that matters never
# gets committed. Adding a line per bump is cheap; debugging that is not.

# Superseded stress tests. Same argument -- QB_stress_test.xlsx is the
# current one, and it is recomputed from the board's Adj PPG on every
# rebuild (see BUILD_RUNBOOK.md step 3).
QB59_stress_test.xlsx

